## What was wrong

With `sortTailwindcss` on, strings in the arguments of a nested non-Tailwind call are kept as is, deliberately unlike prettier-plugin-tailwindcss (tailwindlabs/prettier-plugin-tailwindcss#426).

Templates there were still sorted:

```tsx
// experimentalTailwindcss: { functions: ["cn"] }

// Input
cn(x.includes(`a  b`) && "p-4  flex");
cn(x.includes(`${"a  b"}  c`));
<div className={x.split(`a  b`).length ? "p-4  flex" : ""} />;

// Before
cn(x.includes(`a b`) && "flex p-4");
cn(x.includes(`${"a b"} c`));
<div className={x.split(`a b`).length ? "flex p-4" : ""} />;

// After
cn(x.includes(`a  b`) && "flex p-4");
cn(x.includes(`${"a  b"}  c`));
<div className={x.split(`a  b`).length ? "flex p-4" : ""} />;
```

Cause: the nested call set a `disabled` flag on the current context entry, but only the string literal path checked it.
`TemplateElement` and the layout checks read the entry without it, and the entries pushed for `${}` started with `disabled: false`.

